\begin{abstract}
We develop a rigorous transcript-based framework for the thermodynamic cost of comparison
sorting and use it to settle five questions about the interaction between query complexity
and Landauer erasure. Modelling an algorithm by the map sending each input ordering to the
sequence of query answers it observes, correctness is equivalent to injectivity of that
map, since the sorted output is constant across inputs. From this single structural fact we
obtain: (i) a \emph{multiway depth bound}, that any correct sorter of $n$ items using
queries of radix at most $q$ requires depth at least $\lceil\log_q(n!)\rceil$, together
with achievability of that depth in the transcript model; (ii) \emph{radix independence of
the work ledger}, namely that charging $kT\log q$ per fully erased query register yields a
total at least $kT\log(n!)$ for every $q$, and strictly less than $kT\log(n!)+kT\log q$ for
the depth-optimal sorter, so that changing the query radix trades depth against information
per query without moving the reversible information balance; (iii) a \emph{reset-register
theorem}, that the true Landauer cost of clearing the transcript is $kT$ times the
logarithm of the cardinality of the transcript's image, hence exactly $kT\log(n!)$ for any
correct sorter, independent of depth and radix, and invariant under duplication of the
register, so that logically correlated queries are thermodynamically free; (iv) a
\emph{thermodynamic direct-sum theorem} for independent blocks, in which the erased
information is additive while the reversible history space has cardinality at least the
product $m!\,n!$, with equality exactly when the history map is bijective---a precise
characterisation of garbage-free protocols; and (v) a \emph{fluctuation penalty}, that a
finite-time stochastic protocol obeying the Jarzynski equality with baseline $F$ has
$\langle W\rangle\ge F$, with strict inequality whenever the work is nonconstant on the
support, and with excess exactly $kT\,D(p\|p^{R})$. Finally we prove a
\emph{prior-sensitive} refinement: for any positive prior $p$ on orderings, every correct
self-delimiting comparison sorter has expected comparison count at least $H(p)$, this is
achievable to within one comparison uniformly in $n$, and $H(p)\le\log_2(n!)$ with equality
exactly at the uniform prior---identifying the classical factorial baseline as the
maximum-entropy special case.
\end{abstract}

\noindent\textbf{Keywords:} comparison sorting, Landauer's principle, Jarzynski equality,
decision tree depth, radix, Kraft inequality, Shannon entropy, reversible computation,
relative entropy, direct sum.

\section{Introduction}

\subsection{Two costs of sorting}

The classical information-theoretic lower bound for comparison sorting is a counting
argument. A deterministic algorithm using binary comparisons induces a binary decision
tree; correctness requires a distinct leaf for each of the $n!$ input orderings; a tree of
depth $d$ has at most $2^{d}$ leaves; hence $d\ge\lceil\log_2(n!)\rceil$. This is a
statement about \emph{time}.

There is a second, physically distinct cost. A machine that sorts must end in a state that
no longer distinguishes among the $n!$ possible input orderings. Landauer's principle
asserts that logically irreversible destruction of distinguishability requires minimum work
\[
  W_{\min}=kT\log(\text{number of distinguishable states collapsed}),
\]
so sorting has ideal thermodynamic price $kT\log(n!)$. This is a statement about
\emph{heat}.

The two are frequently conflated because both are logarithms of $n!$. We show that they are
governed by different mathematical objects and respond to different perturbations. Time is
controlled by the \emph{capacity} of the query tree; heat by the \emph{cardinality of the
image} of the transcript map. The first depends on radix, branching and adaptivity; the
second on none of these.

\subsection{The transcript model}

Fix $n\in\mathbb{N}$ and let $\Sn$ denote the set of input orderings, $|\Sn|=n!$.

\begin{definition}[Transcript]
For $q,d\in\mathbb{N}$, a \emph{transcript} of depth $d$ and radix $q$ is a word of length
$d$ over a $q$-letter alphabet. The set of such transcripts is $\mathcal{T}(q,d)$, and
$|\mathcal{T}(q,d)|=q^{d}$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Correct radix-$q$, depth-$d$ sorter]
A \emph{sorter} $S$ for $n$ items is a map $T_S:\Sn\to\mathcal{T}(q,d)$ recording the
sequence of $d$ query answers observed on a given input ordering, subject to the
correctness condition that $T_S$ be injective.
\end{definition}

The injectivity condition carries the whole argument. The output of a sorting algorithm is
the sorted list, identical for every input ordering, and therefore carries zero information
about the input. Consequently the only place the input's identity can reside, in a machine
that applied the correct inverse permutation, is the sequence of answers it received; two
distinct orderings with an identical transcript would drive the algorithm through identical
states. Injectivity of the transcript map is thus necessary for correctness, and it is
exactly the information a physical implementation must eventually reset.

Definition~1.2 is a \emph{relaxation}: it does not require the $k$-th query to be an order
query actually available on $n$ items, nor to be a function of previous answers. Lower
bounds in this model apply a fortiori to genuine adaptive comparison algorithms; upper
bounds hold in the abstract model and are discussed critically in \S8.1.

Throughout, $\log$ is the natural logarithm and $\log_2$ the binary logarithm; one nat of
erasure costs $kT$ and one bit costs $kT\log2$.

\section{Multiway comparisons and optimal radix}

\begin{lemma}[Transcript count]\label{lem:count}
$|\mathcal{T}(q,d)|=q^{d}$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
A transcript is a function from a $d$-element set to a $q$-element set.
\end{proof}

\begin{proposition}[Counting bound]\label{prop:counting}
If $S$ is a correct radix-$q$, depth-$d$ sorter for $n$ items, then $n!\le q^{d}$.
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}
$T_S$ is injective between finite sets, so $|\Sn|\le|\mathcal{T}(q,d)|$; apply $|\Sn|=n!$
and Lemma~\ref{lem:count}.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Multiway depth lower bound]\label{thm:depth}
Let $q\ge2$. Every correct radix-$q$ sorter for $n$ items has depth
$d\ge\lceil\log_q(n!)\rceil$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
For $q\ge2$ the ceiling logarithm satisfies the adjunction $\lceil\log_q m\rceil\le d
\iff m\le q^{d}$; apply it to $m=n!$ using Proposition~\ref{prop:counting}.
\end{proof}

Specialising to $q=2$ recovers $d\ge\lceil\log_2(n!)\rceil=\Theta(n\log n)$.

\begin{theorem}[Achievability]\label{thm:achieve}
For every $n$ and every $q\ge2$ there is a correct radix-$q$ sorter of depth exactly
$\lceil\log_q(n!)\rceil$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
By definition of the ceiling logarithm, $n!\le q^{\lceil\log_q(n!)\rceil}
=|\mathcal{T}(q,\lceil\log_q(n!)\rceil)|$. A pair of finite sets with $|A|\le|B|$ admits an
injection $A\hookrightarrow B$; take $T_S$ to be one.
\end{proof}

\begin{proposition}[Depth is antitone in the radix]
For $2\le q\le q'$, $\lceil\log_{q'}(n!)\rceil\le\lceil\log_q(n!)\rceil$.
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}
If $n!\le q^{d}$ and $q\le q'$ then $n!\le q'^{\,d}$.
\end{proof}

\begin{example}[Verified table, $n=5$, $5!=120$]
\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{lccccc}
\toprule
$q$ & $2$ & $3$ & $4$ & $5$ & $10$\\
\midrule
$\lceil\log_q 120\rceil$ & $7$ & $5$ & $4$ & $3$ & $3$\\
$d\log q$ (nats) & $4.852$ & $5.493$ & $5.545$ & $4.828$ & $6.908$\\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{center}
with $\log120=4.787$. Every ledger entry lies in $[\log120,\ \log120+\log q)$, as
guaranteed by Theorem~\ref{thm:sandwich}.
\end{example}

\section{The physical work ledger}

\begin{definition}[Naive transcript work]
A radix-$q$ query register holds $\log q$ nats. Charging every query register in full, a
depth-$d$ run costs $\Wnaive(kT,q,d)=d\cdot kT\log q$.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Radix independence of the work lower bound]\label{thm:radixindep}
Let $kT\ge0$ and let $S$ be a correct radix-$q$, depth-$d$ sorter. Then
$kT\log(n!)\le\Wnaive(kT,q,d)$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
By Proposition~\ref{prop:counting}, $n!\le q^{d}$; since $n!>0$ and $\log$ is monotone,
$\log(n!)\le d\log q$. Multiply by $kT\ge0$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Optimal-radix sandwich]\label{thm:sandwich}
Let $q\ge2$, $n\ge2$, $kT>0$ and $d^{\star}=\lceil\log_q(n!)\rceil$. Then
\[
  kT\log(n!)\ \le\ \Wnaive(kT,q,d^{\star})\ <\ kT\log(n!)+kT\log q.
\]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
The left inequality is Theorem~\ref{thm:radixindep} applied to a sorter of depth
$d^{\star}$, which exists by Theorem~\ref{thm:achieve}. For the right inequality,
$n!\ge n\ge2>1$, so $d^{\star}\ge1$ and the ceiling logarithm satisfies
$q^{\,d^{\star}-1}<n!$. Taking logarithms, $(d^{\star}-1)\log q<\log(n!)$, i.e.
$d^{\star}\log q<\log(n!)+\log q$; multiply by $kT>0$.
\end{proof}

Thus the optimal-depth ledger is pinned to a window of width exactly one query register
above the Landauer baseline, for every radix: changing the query radix does not alter the
reversible information balance of sorting.

\section{Reset registers: cost is image entropy, not transcript length}

\begin{definition}[Reset work]
Let $A$ be a finite input set and $T:A\to\tau$ a register-valued observable. The work to
reset the register to a fixed blank state is $\Wreset(kT,T)=kT\log|T(A)|$.
\end{definition}

Because the sorted output is constant, $\log|T(A)|$ is the conditional entropy of the
transcript given the output; this is what a Landauer accounting must charge.

\begin{theorem}[Correlated registers are free]\label{thm:dup}
For any $T:A\to\tau$, $\Wreset\bigl(kT,\ a\mapsto(T(a),T(a))\bigr)=\Wreset(kT,T)$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
The image of $a\mapsto(T(a),T(a))$ is the image of $T(A)$ under the injective diagonal
$t\mapsto(t,t)$, hence has the same cardinality.
\end{proof}

\begin{proposition}[The naive charge dominates]
For every $T:\Sn\to\mathcal{T}(q,d)$ and $kT\ge0$,
$\Wreset(kT,T)\le\Wnaive(kT,q,d)$.
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}
$T(\Sn)\subseteq\mathcal{T}(q,d)$ is nonempty, so $1\le|T(\Sn)|\le q^{d}$; take logarithms
and multiply by $kT$.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}\label{lem:image}
If $S$ is a correct radix-$q$, depth-$d$ sorter then $|T_S(\Sn)|=n!$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{theorem}[Reset-register theorem]\label{thm:reset}
For every correct radix-$q$, depth-$d$ sorter $S$ and every $kT$,
\[
  \Wreset(kT,T_S)=kT\log(n!),
\]
exactly the Landauer gap of the sorting task; in particular the reset cost is independent
of depth $d$ and radix $q$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Combine the definition of $\Wreset$ with Lemma~\ref{lem:image}.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Synthesis for the reset conjecture]
For a correct radix-$q$, depth-$d$ sorter and $kT>0$:
(1) $\lceil\log_q(n!)\rceil\le d$;
(2) $kT\log(n!)\le\Wnaive(kT,q,d)$;
(3) $\Wreset(kT,T_S)=kT\log(n!)$, independent of $d$ and $q$;
(4) duplicating the transcript register changes nothing.
\end{corollary}

This resolves the conjecture that the dissipated work of a comparator network is governed
by the conditional entropy of the comparison transcript given the sorted output rather than
by the transcript length: items (3) and (4) are exactly that statement, and (2) shows that
length-based accounting is a valid but generally loose upper model. Redundant padding of a
comparator network is precisely the case where the two accountings diverge without bound
while the true cost is constant.

\section{A thermodynamic direct-sum theorem}

\begin{theorem}[Additivity of erased information]
For finite nonempty $A,B$, collapsing $A\times B$ to a point erases the sum of the
information erased by collapsing $A$ and by collapsing $B$. For block sorting,
\[
  W_{\min}(m\oplus n)=kT\log(m!)+kT\log(n!).
\]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Erased information is $\log_2(|A|\,|B|)=\log_2|A|+\log_2|B|$; specialise
$A=S_m$, $B=S_n$.
\end{proof}

Now let $e:S_m\times S_n\xrightarrow{\ \sim\ }\{\ast\}\times\Aux$ be a reversible
implementation with $\Aux$ finite, and define the \emph{history map}
$h_e(p)=\pi_2(e(p))$.

\begin{lemma}\label{lem:hinj}
$h_e$ is injective.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
$h_e(p_1)=h_e(p_2)$ forces $e(p_1)=e(p_2)$; $e$ is injective.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[History states multiply]
$|\Aux|\ge m!\cdot n!$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Lemma~\ref{lem:hinj} and $|S_m\times S_n|=m!\,n!$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Equality case: no cross-block garbage]\label{thm:equality}
$|\Aux|=m!\,n!$ if and only if $h_e$ is a bijection.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
($\Rightarrow$) An injective map between finite sets of equal cardinality is bijective.
($\Leftarrow$) A bijection between finite sets forces equal cardinalities.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Strict garbage bound]
If $h_e$ fails to be surjective then $m!\,n!<|\Aux|$ strictly.
\end{corollary}

Entropy adds while reversible state counts multiply: the same fact on the two sides of a
logarithm, but with different physical roles---the additive quantity is a \emph{cost}, the
multiplicative one a \emph{resource}. Theorem~\ref{thm:equality} makes ``garbage-free'' a
checkable property.

\begin{theorem}[Depth direct sum]
Let $S$ be a joint radix-$q$, depth-$d$ sorter for two independent blocks, i.e.\ an
injective transcript map on $S_m\times S_n$, and $kT\ge0$. Then
$kT\log(m!)+kT\log(n!)\le\Wnaive(kT,q,d)$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Injectivity gives $m!\,n!\le q^{d}$; take logarithms and use additivity of $\log$.
\end{proof}

\section{The fluctuation penalty above the sorting baseline}

\begin{definition}[Work ensemble]
Let $\Omega$ be finite. A \emph{work ensemble} consists of $p:\Omega\to\mathbb{R}$ with
$p_i>0$ (so $\Omega$ \emph{is} the support) and $\sum_i p_i=1$, and $W:\Omega\to\mathbb{R}$.
Its expected work is $\langle W\rangle=\sum_i p_iW_i$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Jarzynski equality; reverse weights]
The ensemble satisfies the Jarzynski equality with baseline $F$ at $kT\neq0$ if
$\sum_i p_ie^{-W_i/kT}=e^{-F/kT}$. The reverse weights are
$p_i^{R}=p_ie^{-(W_i-F)/kT}$, and
$D(p\|p^{R})=\sum_i p_i\log(p_i/p_i^{R})$.
\end{definition}

\begin{lemma}[Jarzynski $=$ normalisation of the reverse process]\label{lem:norm}
If the Jarzynski equality holds with baseline $F$, then $\sum_i p_i^{R}=1$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
$p_i^{R}=(p_ie^{-W_i/kT})e^{F/kT}$, so the sum is $e^{-F/kT}e^{F/kT}=1$.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[Log-ratio identity]\label{lem:ratio}
$\log(p_i/p_i^{R})=(W_i-F)/kT$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{theorem}[Second law for the ensemble]\label{thm:secondlaw}
If $kT>0$ and the Jarzynski equality holds with baseline $F$, then
$F\le\langle W\rangle$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Set $x_i=e^{-(W_i-F)/kT}>0$. Pointwise $\log x_i\le x_i-1$; weight by $p_i>0$ and sum. The
right side is $\sum_i p_i^{R}-1=0$ by Lemma~\ref{lem:norm}; the left side is
$(F-\langle W\rangle)/kT$. Hence $F-\langle W\rangle\le0$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Strict fluctuation penalty]\label{thm:strict}
Under the hypotheses of Theorem~\ref{thm:secondlaw}, if some trajectory in the support has
$W_j\neq F$---in particular if the work distribution is nonconstant on the support---then
$F<\langle W\rangle$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
$\log x\le x-1$ is strict for $x\neq1$, and $x_j\neq1$ iff $W_j\neq F$ (using $kT>0$). One
strict term with positive weight makes the summed inequality strict. If all $W_i=F$ the
work is constant, so nonconstancy supplies such a $j$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Dissipation is exactly a divergence]\label{thm:diverge}
For $kT>0$ and any baseline $F$,
$\langle W\rangle-F=kT\,D(p\|p^{R})$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
By Lemma~\ref{lem:ratio}, $D(p\|p^{R})=\sum_i p_i(W_i-F)/kT=(\langle W\rangle-F)/kT$.
\end{proof}

The identity holds for any $F$; the Jarzynski equality is what makes $p^{R}$ normalised and
hence $D$ a genuine (nonnegative) relative entropy, recovering
Theorem~\ref{thm:secondlaw} a second way.

\begin{theorem}[Fluctuation penalty for sorting]
Let $kT>0$ and let a finite-time stochastic implementation of sorting $n$ items satisfy the
Jarzynski equality with the exact Landauer baseline $F=kT\log(n!)$. If the work
distribution is nonconstant on its support, then
\[
  \langle W\rangle>kT\log(n!),\qquad
  \langle W\rangle-kT\log(n!)=kT\,D(p\|p^{R}).
\]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Theorems~\ref{thm:strict} and \ref{thm:diverge} with $F=kT\log(n!)$.
\end{proof}

\begin{example}
Take $n=3$, $kT=1$, $F=\log6=1.7918$, $\Omega=\{1,2\}$, $p=(\tfrac12,\tfrac12)$. Choose
$W_1=\log6-0.5=1.2917$, so $e^{-W_1}=0.27473$; Jarzynski forces
$e^{-W_2}=\tfrac13-0.27473=0.05860$, $W_2=2.8379$. Then $\langle W\rangle=2.0648$, an
excess of $0.2731$, while $p^{R}=(0.8242,0.1758)$ and
$D(p\|p^{R})=\tfrac12\log\frac{0.5}{0.8242}+\tfrac12\log\frac{0.5}{0.1758}=0.2731$.
\end{example}

\begin{remark}
Positivity of $p$ is load-bearing: it encodes ``nonconstant \emph{on the support}''. A
deterministic protocol satisfying Jarzynski must have $W\equiv F$, giving equality---the
quasistatic limit.
\end{remark}

\section{Prior-sensitive sorting}

\begin{definition}[Prior-sensitive comparison sorter]
A \emph{prior sorter} for $n$ items is a map $c:\Sn\to\{0,1\}^{*}$ assigning to each input
ordering the binary transcript of the comparisons performed, such that $c$ is injective
(correctness) and $c(\Sn)$ is \emph{prefix-free}.
\end{definition}

Prefix-freeness is exactly the statement that the algorithm can tell from the answers alone
that it has finished, without an external clock. An algorithm consulting an external clock
is not Kraft-constrained; for such models only the fixed-depth bound
$\lceil\log_2(n!)\rceil$ of Theorem~\ref{thm:depth} applies.

\begin{theorem}[Entropy lower bound]
Let $p$ be a prior on $\Sn$ with $p_\sigma>0$ and $\sum_\sigma p_\sigma=1$. Every prior
sorter $c$ satisfies $H(p)\le\mathbb{E}_p[\,|c(\sigma)|\,]$, where
$H(p)=-\sum_\sigma p_\sigma\log_2 p_\sigma$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Injectivity plus prefix-freeness gives Kraft's inequality
$\sum_\sigma2^{-|c(\sigma)|}\le1$; Shannon's source coding lower bound follows from the
Gibbs inequality applied to $p$ and the sub-normalised weights $2^{-|c(\sigma)|}$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Achievability within one comparison]
For every such prior there is a prior sorter $c$ with
$H(p)\le\mathbb{E}_p[\,|c(\sigma)|\,]<H(p)+1$. Its transcript \emph{is} its retained
reversible history, so the history is compressed to the same $H(p)$ scale.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Shannon--Fano lengths $\ell_\sigma=\lceil-\log_2p_\sigma\rceil$ satisfy Kraft's inequality;
its converse produces an injective prefix-free code with those lengths, and
$\mathbb{E}_p[\ell]<H(p)+1$.
\end{proof}

The additive slack is a \emph{single} comparison, uniformly in $n$.

\begin{lemma}[Gibbs pointwise estimate]\label{lem:gibbs}
For $a,b>0$, $a(\log b-\log a)\le b-a$, strictly when $a\neq b$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
$\log(b/a)\le b/a-1$, strict for $b/a\neq1$; multiply by $a>0$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Maximum entropy]\label{thm:maxent}
For any positive normalised $p$ on a finite set of size $N$, $H(p)\le\log_2N$, with
equality iff $p$ is uniform.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Apply Lemma~\ref{lem:gibbs} with $a=p_i$, $b=1/N$ and sum: the right side telescopes to
$0$, the left equals $-\log N-\sum_i p_i\log p_i$. Hence $-\sum_ip_i\log p_i\le\log N$;
divide by $\log2$. Strictness at one index propagates to the sum; the uniform case is a
direct computation.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}
For any positive prior on the $n!$ orderings, $H(p)\le\log_2(n!)$, with equality precisely
at the uniform prior.
\end{corollary}

\begin{theorem}[Prior-sensitive work below the uniform baseline]
Let $kT>0$. For every positive prior $p$ on $\Sn$ there is a prior sorter whose expected
comparison count is within one comparison of $H(p)$ and whose expected reset work satisfies
\[
  kT\log2\cdot\mathbb{E}_p[\,|c|\,]<kT\log(n!)+kT\log2 .
\]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
$\mathbb{E}_p[|c|]<H(p)+1\le\log_2(n!)+1$; multiply by $kT\log2$ and use
$\log2\cdot\log_2(n!)=\log(n!)$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Biased priors are strictly cheaper]
If $p$ is not uniform on $\Sn$ and $kT>0$, then
$kT\log2\cdot H(p)<kT\log(n!)$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Theorem~\ref{thm:maxent} gives $H(p)<\log_2(n!)$; multiply by $kT\log2>0$.
\end{proof}

\begin{example}
For $n=3$ with the dyadic prior
$(\tfrac12,\tfrac14,\tfrac18,\tfrac1{16},\tfrac1{32},\tfrac1{32})$,
$H(p)=1.9375$ bits against the uniform $\log_26=2.585$ bits: a saving of $0.647$ bits.
Since all probabilities are dyadic, the optimal prefix code attains $1.9375$ comparisons on
average with zero overshoot.
\end{example}

\section{Algorithms, discussion, and open problems}

\subsection{The comparison-availability gap}

Theorem~\ref{thm:achieve} is proved in the abstract transcript model: it produces an
injection from orderings to transcripts, not an algorithm built from genuine order queries.
Realisable transcripts satisfy an extra \emph{consistency} condition: the fibre of any
prefix is an order ideal cut out by the queries asked so far, and a multiway order query
cannot split a set of permutations into $q$ arbitrary pieces. One therefore expects the
minimal \emph{adaptive} depth to exceed the counting bound $\lceil\log_q(n!)\rceil$ for
infinitely many $(n,q)$. All lower bounds above are unaffected: they use only injectivity.

\subsection{Scope of the reset theorem}

Theorem~\ref{thm:reset} concerns the \emph{retained} register at the moment of erasure. It
does not model which comparisons are physically available at each step, nor the cost of
\emph{performing} a comparison (which can in principle be made reversible and arbitrarily
cheap), nor transient garbage later uncomputed. Its force is in ruling out length-based
accounting: any law of the form ``work $\propto$ number of comparisons'' is falsified by
transcript duplication (Theorem~\ref{thm:dup}).

\subsection{Stability of the equality case}

Theorem~\ref{thm:equality} is an all-or-nothing pigeonhole statement, while its corollary
shows the failure mode is a cardinality \emph{defect} that ought to carry a metric. Define
the \emph{garbage index} $|\Aux|/(m!\,n!)\ge1$; one may ask whether protocols within a
factor $1+\varepsilon$ of the minimum have history maps surjective onto a
$(1-\varepsilon)$-fraction of $\Aux$, and whether the number of elementary uncomputation
steps needed to reach the garbage-free case is $\Theta(\log(\text{garbage index}))$. Both
endpoints are established here; the interpolating stability statement is open and
falsifiable by exhaustive search at $(m,n)=(3,2)$.

\subsection{Toward a fluctuation--depth uncertainty relation}

Theorem~\ref{thm:diverge} gives the dissipation exactly but says nothing about how
$D(p\|p^{R})$ scales with the speed of the protocol. For a protocol executing $d$
comparisons with per-comparison relaxation time $\tau$, one expects a trade-off between
excess work and total time---an uncertainty relation between dissipation and depth. The
exact baseline $kT\log(n!)$ makes such a relation cleanly testable.

\subsection{Algorithmic kernels}

Three computational kernels are implicit above. (i) \emph{Optimal multiway depth}: find the
least $d$ with $q^{d}\ge n!$ by exact integer arithmetic, costing $O(d\,M(\log n!))$ time
with $M$ the big-integer multiplication cost. (ii) \emph{Ledger and sandwich certificate}:
for each radix, report $d^{\star}$, the ledger $d^{\star}\log q$ and the interval
$[\log(n!),\log(n!)+\log q)$, computing $\log(n!)=\sum_{k=2}^{n}\log k$ in floating point
while deciding $d^{\star}$ exactly. (iii) \emph{Jarzynski-consistent ensemble
construction}: given $p$, $F$, $kT$ and free choices $W_1,\dots,W_{r-1}$, solve
$e^{-W_r/kT}=p_r^{-1}\bigl(e^{-F/kT}-\sum_{i<r}p_ie^{-W_i/kT}\bigr)$, admissible exactly
when the bracket is positive; the construction is $O(r)$ and certifies
$\langle W\rangle-F=kT\,D(p\|p^{R})$.

\subsection{Applications}

\emph{Reversible hardware.} The designer's target is the image of the retained register,
not the length of the log: keep whatever intermediate results you like, provided they are
deterministic functions of a small retained core.
\emph{Radix selection.} A higher comparator radix is a pure latency optimisation with no
thermodynamic penalty or bonus beyond one query register.
\emph{Data-aware sorting.} A good prior is worth $\log_2(n!)-H(p)$ comparisons and
$kT(\log(n!)-\log2\cdot H(p))$ joules, achievable up to one comparison.
\emph{Blocked sorting.} Heat adds across blocks while the reversible state space
multiplies; cross-block bookkeeping shows up as a strictly larger history space.

\subsection{Summary}

The unifying moral is a separation of two logarithms of $n!$. The depth
$\lceil\log_q(n!)\rceil$ concerns \emph{tree capacity} and is sensitive to the query model.
The work $kT\log(n!)$ concerns \emph{image cardinality} and is insensitive to depth, radix
and redundancy---but sensitive to the prior, to independence structure, and to speed. Every
result above is an instance of one of these two logarithms.

